ue above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:  \nYou hold **A♠ 10♠**, and the board is **4♠ 5♣ 6♦ 4♥ 2♥**. This is the **river** (fifth and final community card), so no more cards will come.\n\nLet’s analyze your hand and possible holdings:\n\n- You have **Ace-high with a pair of 4s on the board**. Your best hand is **One Pair (4s)**, with an Ace kicker.\n- There are **no flushes possible for you**: you have two spades (A♠, 10♠), but only **one other spade** on the board (4♠). That makes just three spades — not enough for a flush.\n- No straight is possible with your hole cards: The board has 4, 5, 6 — so a 3 and 7 would make a straight, but you don’t have either. A-10 doesn't connect.\n- You do **not** have trips or two pair. The board pairs the 4s, but you don’t hold another 4.\n\nSo your actual hand is **a single pair of 4s, with A-K-Q-J-10 kickers available** — your best five-card hand is **4♠ 4♥ A♠ K (if K exists), Q, etc.** — but since no other broadway cards are on board, your **best five-card hand is: 6♦, 5♣, 4♠, 4♥, A♠** — one pair.\n\nNow consider what your opponent might have:\n- The board is low and paired (two 4s), with little draw potential.\n- If they called all three of your raises on previous streets (pre-flop, flop, turn), they likely have a decent hand or a draw they believed in.\n- Possible hands they could have that beat yours:\n  - Any **overpair** (like 55, 66, 77+), though only 55 and 66 hit the board.\n  - **Two pair**: 5-6, 4-5, 4-6, or even 5-4 (though unlikely).\n  - **Trips**: 4-4 (they hold 4), 5-5, 6-6.\n  - **Straight draws completed?** No — the straight would require 3-7, which isn’t present.\n  - **Flush draws?** Only three spades total, so no flush possible unless someone held two spades and there are three on board — but only one spade (4♠) is on board. Wait — correction: **you hold two spades**, and **4♠** is on board — that’s **three spades**: 4♠, A♠, 10♠ → yes, three spades on boa